{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader lord who lived during the 12th century his tenure as Count of Tripoli marked a period of significant political and military activity in the Levant. Born around 1140 he was the son of Raymond II of Tripolis and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His family was one of the most prominent in the Crusader states with deep roots in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 at the age of twelve. His early years were dominated by the influence of his mother Hodierna who served as regent until his majority. During this time Tripoli faced numerous challenges including internal strife and external threats from Muslim powers particularly Nur ad-Din a powerful Zengid ruler. Raymond’s upbringing was marked by the political instability of the region and the constant need to defend against invasions. His mother’s role in governance was crucial as she navigated these turbulent times ensuring the county’s survival until Raymond came of age.\n\nIn the late 1150s Raymond emerged as a capable ruler he was known for his diplomatic skills and military prowess. He inherited a county that was strategically important but often at odds with its neighbors. His reign saw efforts to strengthen Tripoli’s position through alliances and military campaigns. One of his first major actions was to secure the border with the Principality of Antioch a neighboring Crusader state plagued by conflicts with Muslim neighbors.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Marriage and Family**\nRaymond’s personal life was intertwined with the political landscape of the Crusader states. He married Eschiva of Bures who was the widow of Joscelin II Count of Edessa. Joscelin had been killed in 1150 and his death left Eschiva and her young son Baldwin in a precarious position. Raymond’s marriage to Eschiva was likely influenced by political considerations aiming to strengthen his alliances and secure the loyalty of key figures within the Crusader states. The union produced no children which may have complicated Raymond’s succession plans.\n\nEschiva’s background added another layer to Raymond’s political maneuvering she brought connections to other Crusader families and her previous marriage to Joscelin added weight to Raymond’s claims in the region. Despite the lack of heirs Raymond’s rule was stable and he maintained strong ties with other Crusader leaders including Baldwin III of Jerusalem and Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Military Campaigns and Later Years**\nRaymond III’s reign was defined by his military engagement particularly in the conflict against Nur ad-Din. In 1164 he participated in a Crusader raid on Homs a key Zengid stronghold near Antioch. This campaign though initially successful ultimately failed to achieve lasting results as Nur ad-Din’s forces managed to repel the Crusaders in the following years. Raymond also played a role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem serving as a regent for Baldwin IV when the young king became ill with leprosy in 1174.\n\nBy the late 1170s Raymond’s health began to decline and he grew increasingly reliant on advisors. His final years were marked by a shift in the balance of power in the region with the rise of Saladin who would go on toUnity the Muslim forces and challenge the Crusader states. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly before the Battle of Hattin a devastating defeat for the Crusaders that led to the loss of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond’s legacy lies in his efforts to maintain Tripoli’s independence and his contributions to the defense of the Crusader states. His reign spanned a critical period in the history of the Levant and his actions reflected the challenges faced by the Crusaders in maintaining their presence in the Holy Land. Despite the eventua